EXT. MANGROVE SWAMP - TIDAL FLAT - DAWN

Roots like fingers. Grey mud. A capsized skiff on its side. Mosquitos in clouds.

BIANCA "BEE" CALLOWAY, 30s, torn shirt, drags a water jug through the mud. BRAM OKONJO, 40s, one boot missing, limps behind her. Between them, a bundled shape on a stretcher of branches: CAPTAIN HALVARD SKOGLUND, 60s, eyes closed.

BEE
Bram. Pull.

BRAM
I'm pulling.

BEE
You're limping.

BRAM
I'm limping and pulling.

She stops. Wipes her face. Squats by the captain. Puts two fingers at his neck.

BEE
Cap. Cap, you in there?

He stirs. Mumbles. Doesn't wake.

BRAM
Tide's turning.

BEE
I can see.

BRAM
We have maybe ten minutes before the flat goes under.

She stands. Looks at the skiff. Looks at the horizon where a thin line of silver crawls toward them.

BEE
Leave the jug.

BRAM
That's our water.

BEE
And that's his life. Choose.

Bram stares at her. Drops the jug. It sinks into the mud with a slow burp. He grabs the stretcher. She grabs the other end. They lift. They stumble. A root catches Bram's foot; he falls. The stretcher tilts. The captain groans.

BEE (CONT'D)
Bram!

BRAM
Go on without me.

BEE
Not a chance.

She sets the stretcher down. Hauls him up by the belt. He leans on her. Their breath rasps.

BRAM
Bee. If I --

BEE
You're not going to.

BRAM
If I do.

BEE
Don't.

The silver line is closer. The mud begins to glisten. A bird shrieks from the roots.

CAPTAIN HALVARD
(eyes still shut)
Boys. Girls. Hold your horses.

BEE
Cap!

CAPTAIN HALVARD
I can walk.

BRAM
You can't.

CAPTAIN HALVARD
I can crawl. Let me.

He pushes himself off the stretcher. Falls to hands and knees. Starts to crawl. Bee and Bram watch him go, mud to the elbows.

BEE
(quiet)
Stubborn old man.

BRAM
He taught us.

They follow. The water laps their ankles. The roots rise ahead like a gate.
